\section*{Question 5}
![](https://cdn.mathpix.com/cropped/d68f5a3d-8118-4475-83cb-15a009549294-1.jpg?height=672&width=653&top_left_y=374&top_left_x=735)
The inside of a funnel of height 10 inches has circular cross sections, as shown in the figure above. At height \(h\), the radius of the funnel is given by \(r=\frac{1}{20}\left(3+h^{2}\right)\), where \(0 \leq h \leq 10\). The units of \(r\) and \(h\) are inches.
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] Find the average value of the radius of the funnel.
\item[(b)] Find the volume of the funnel.
\item[(c)] The funnel contains liquid that is draining from the bottom. At the instant when the height of the liquid is \(h=3\) inches, the radius of the surface of the liquid is decreasing at a rate of \(\frac{1}{5}\) inch per second. At this instant, what is the rate of change of the height of the liquid with respect to time?
\item[(a)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Average radius } & =\frac{1}{10} \int_{0}^{10} \frac{1}{20}\left(3+h^{2}\right) d h=\frac{1}{200}\left[3 h+\frac{h^{3}}{3}\right]_{0}^{10} \\
& =\frac{1}{200}\left(\left(30+\frac{1000}{3}\right)-0\right)=\frac{109}{60} \mathrm{in}
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { integral } \\
1: \text { antiderivative } \\
1: \text { answer }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(b)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text { Volume } & =\pi \int_{0}^{10}\left(\left(\frac{1}{20}\right)\left(3+h^{2}\right)\right)^{2} d h=\frac{\pi}{400} \int_{0}^{10}\left(9+6 h^{2}+h^{4}\right) d h \\
& =\frac{\pi}{400}\left[9 h+2 h^{3}+\frac{h^{5}}{5}\right]_{0}^{10} \\
& =\frac{\pi}{400}\left(\left(90+2000+\frac{100000}{5}\right)-0\right)=\frac{2209 \pi}{40} \mathrm{in}^{3}
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
1: \text { integrand } \\
1: \text { antiderivative } \\
1: \text { answer }
\end{array}\right.
\]
\begin{itemize}
\item[(c)]
\[
\begin{aligned}
& \frac{d r}{d t}=\frac{1}{20}(2 h) \frac{d h}{d t} \\
& -\frac{1}{5}=\frac{3}{10} \frac{d h}{d t} \\
& \frac{d h}{d t}=-\frac{1}{5} \cdot \frac{10}{3}=-\frac{2}{3} \mathrm{in} / \mathrm{sec}
\end{aligned}
\]
\end{itemize}
\[
3:\left\{\begin{array}{l}
2: \text { chain rule } \\
1: \text { answer }
\end{array}\right.
\]
